addclaim(22,906,1,list(range(4,11)),'5–263','Paraphrase: a small marked category has a small universal completion under prescribed small families of limits, colimits and exactness tests.','For a small sketch and a set of bounded diagram shapes, free completion and forcing designated comparisons are small constructions in a sufficiently large universe. The particular mixed exactness doctrine additionally needs the stated compatibility and successor universal properties.','construction sketched; the generalized successor construction K is explicitly left unwritten','Adjoin formal cone objects, factorization arrows and uniqueness relations in regular-cardinal stages. With a regular bound larger than all arities, every operation and relation occurs before a common later stage. This supplies the bounded term/sketch construction; it does not prove unspecified large or incompatible mixed doctrines have the same envelope.','Lack–Tendas Theorem 4.4 identifies accessible categories with small sketches, and Proposition 5.12/Corollaries 5.13–5.14 give exact model-category hypotheses. Kelly’s free-cocompletion theory gives the bounded one-sided completions. The manuscript’s universal mixed 2-doctrine is only matched under these explicit conditions.',['LackTendas2023','Kelly1982'],'C')
addclaim(23,906,1,[6,10,16],'102–124;253–258;436–461','Paraphrase: take a cardinal π larger than all diagram cardinalities; the transfinite union then captures every diagram.','The capture argument requires cofinality larger than every indexing size, achieved by a sufficiently large regular cardinal. Cardinal inequality alone is insufficient.','false as first written; wholly canceled repetition on scan16; repaired by file02:453–462','At π=aleph_omega a countable list of stages aleph_n is unbounded, although omega<π. With regular κ, fewer than κ stages have a common bound; this captures diagrams, natural transformations, inverse arrows and equality witnesses. File02 later explicitly asks for an index set grand devant the bound.','The sharply-larger/regular-cardinal framework is explicit in Lack–Tendas §§2.1–2.3. The concrete cofinality counterexample and the repaired transfinite proof are checked here.',['LackTendas2023'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-023')
addclaim(24,906,1,[11,12,13,14],'268–416','Paraphrase: full faithfulness and faithfulness of restriction should be expressible by extension conditions on marked categories.','For a small f:R→R′, fullness of Fun(R′,E)→Fun(R,E) is encoded by extending maps from the glued two-endpoint cylinder S=R′⊔_R(R×[1])⊔_R R′ to R′×[1]; faithfulness is encoded by the fold from two such cylinders. Carry the marked cones on the endpoints.','open on the pages','A functor from S specifies two extensions and a transformation of their restrictions; a cylinder extension is precisely a lift of the transformation. Two lifts agree exactly when a functor from the doubled cylinder factors through the fold. Isomorphism invariance converts strict representatives to essential-surjectivity conditions. Composition and intersections of compatible exactness conditions follow by composition and union of tests.','Small sketch and presentation methods in Kelly and Lack–Tendas support the ambient construction; the exact cylinder/fold formulas are independent reconstruction in this run. The manuscript’s compatibility condition is retained.',['Kelly1982','LackTendas2023'])
addclaim(25,906,2,[27,28,29,30,31,32],'294–535','Paraphrase: the second theorem is proved by iterating K; exact cones lift to a stage.','Assuming K satisfies the listed universal and exactness obligations, regular iteration gives the claimed envelope and exact-cone lifting. The text ends before completing the definition of preexactness and never constructs the whole K.','conditional proof sketched; definition unfinished','The underlying small diagram factors at one stage; its required cone is adjoined next. An isomorphism in the colimit is represented by an arrow and inverse, whose two identities hold at a later common stage. Thus exactness is witnessed eventually, up to isomorphism. Duplicating a cone vertex and inverting its comparison tests exactness, but does not alone construct all successor operations.','The exactness-lifting argument is established under regular smallness; the general doctrine is only partly matched by small-sketch theory. No later source found in the searched literature supplies a theorem for the unfinished local notion of preexactness as written.',['LackTendas2023','Kelly1982'],'C')
addclaim(26,907,2,[20,21,22,23],'6–148','Paraphrase: every topos is algebraic, presented by operations, equations and axioms; adjoining operations is a 2-pullback on models.','Every Grothendieck topos classifies a small geometric theory. A small site with finite limits gives the theory of left-exact functors satisfying its covering axioms. Here algebraic includes geometric disjunction/cover axioms, and is not restricted to an equational variety.','proof sketched','Use a small finite-limit presenting site C; PSh(C) classifies Lex(C,-), and each covering family imposes that its image is jointly epimorphic. Compatible extra operations are represented by bicategorical amalgamation and then these axioms. The empty I-sorted theory has classifier PSh((FinSet^I_fin)^op).','SGA4 IV 1.2 and 9.1.2–9.1.7 establish the presheaf and quotient constructions. The original IV9.1.6(d) explicitly records existence of the 2-fiber products used in the relative formulation.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(27,908,2,[24],'161–174','Paraphrase: model categories admit filtered colimits; finite limits may characterize a presheaf classifier.','Geometric models have objectwise filtered colimits. Cartesian models Lex(C,E) have pointwise limits and, for E a Grothendieck topos, a locally presentable category structure; their arbitrary colimits need not be pointwise. The converse based only on existence of limits is a distinct question.','affirmative assertions sketched; converse explicitly queried','Filtered colimits commute with finite limits in a topos and preserve covering axioms. Groups show nonpointwise coproducts: the underlying set of G*H is not the disjoint union. Merely existing limits without specifying their creation and compatibility with all geometric inverse images is weaker than a cartesian presentation.','Makkai–Pitts Theorem 3.3/Corollary 3.4 and Lack–Tendas Proposition 5.12 give precise hypotheses for models; Beke’s presheaf-type criteria show why the converse needs more than a slogan. Searches did not identify the manuscript’s bare existence-only converse as stated.',['MakkaiPitts1987','LackTendas2023','BekePresheafType'],'C')
addclaim(28,907,2,[21,22,23],'43–77;127–148','Paraphrase: the quotient/localization functors are surjective on Hom; the diagram asks loc?','A quotient of term categories can be full and essentially surjective. A left-exact reflector between topoi need not be full. Restriction along a full essentially-surjective ordinary quotient is fully faithful, but its left adjoint is not automatically left exact.','false if applied to the topos reflector; localization question remains in the diagram','On C=[0→1] with the covering {0→1}, PSh(C) has diagrams X1→X0 and sheafification is evaluation at0 followed by the constant-arrow embedding. Let P=(1→1), Q=(empty→1). There is no P→Q, while their sheafifications are both terminal and have one map. The finite Hom enumeration is saved.','SGA4 IV9 describes geometric localization without requiring the reflector to be full. The finite-site counterexample distinguishes the two quotient levels; the ordinary restriction assertion follows directly from naturality along a full quotient.',['SGA4I1972'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-028')
addclaim(29,907,1,[19],'494–524','Paraphrase: geometric localization, small axioms and fully faithful represented theory characterize a subtopos.','For a geometric morphism i:E′→E, all Hom_top(F,E′)→Hom_top(F,E) are fully faithful exactly when i is a geometric inclusion. Subtopoi correspond to localizing topologies, hence to a set of covering axioms on a small presenting site.','stated with a question on the representable full-faithfulness implication','SGA IV9.1.4 proves the forward implication. Exercise9.1.6(b,c) gives the converse for full faithfulness on all 2-cells, with slices as tests; (d) gives base-change stability. This is stronger than injectivity on isomorphism classes or full faithfulness only on invertible 2-cells. Inverting literally every mono, as line520 might say, would invert0→1 and degenerate the topos.','Exact own later published result: SGA4 I, Exposé IV, Proposition9.1.4 printed433; Exercise9.1.6(b,c,d) printed434–435. All three original pages were opened/rendered and read in this run.',['SGA4I1972'],'A',event='targeted proof check')
addclaim(30,908,2,[24,25],'179–249','Paraphrase: a diagram gives six quotient theories of the theory of an object.','The six displayed theories are valid examples: unrestricted, inhabited, subterminal, terminal, empty and inconsistent objects. They are not an exhaustive list of quotient theories.','examples stated; no unambiguous only-six claim','In a topos epi+mono is an isomorphism, giving the displayed intersections. Another quotient imposes that the union of the three diagonals covers X³, with Set models |X|≤2, distinct from all six. The analogous n+1-variable axiom bounds cardinality by n.','The small geometric quotient construction is SGA4 IV9.1. The additional finite-cardinality quotient is a direct syntactic reconstruction; no exhaustive classification is inferred from the diagram.',['SGA4I1972'])
addclaim(31,908,2,[25],'248–264','Paraphrase: what is the subterminal-object classifier, and what are its open/closed relations?','Subterminal objects are classified by the Sierpinski topos. This quotient of the object classifier is neither open nor closed; lattice complements must be taken in the full subtopos lattice.','open questions beside the diagram','A subterminal object is precisely an open of the base topos; a frame map from the three-element Sierpinski frame chooses exactly one such open. Sentences in the empty one-sort geometric theory have only false, inhabited and true truth conditions: existentially quantified equalities have a witness in any inhabited object. Thus its nontrivial open quotient is the inhabited-object theory; the corresponding nontrivial closed quotient is the empty-object theory. The subterminal quotient is neither.','SGA4 IV9.2.1–9.2.6 and9.3.4–9.3.5 give the exact open and complementary closed constructions; IV9.1 gives the quotient lattice. The Sierpinski frame argument is independently checked here. The finite-diagram complement guesses are not promoted to a full lattice calculation.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')
addclaim(32,908,2,[25],'265–273','Paraphrase: constant, locally constant and constructible objects are considered and excluded from the preceding pattern.','Local constancy and constancy of objects, with all ambient maps, need not be closed under filtered colimits and therefore are not in general geometric full subtheories. Constructibility requires an explicit finiteness convention.','fragmentary negative observations','On Cantor space choose p and increasing clopens U_n whose union is C minus{p}. The subterminal sheaves U_n are locally constant, but their union is not. For constant objects use the constant two-point sheaf with successive maps collapsing the second point on U_n; the colimit has two stalk elements at p and one elsewhere, hence is not locally constant.','Geometric models’ filtered-colimit closure follows from SGA4’s finite-limit/cover axioms; these fully specified topological examples refute automatic geometric definability. No undefined constructibility convention is supplied.',['SGA4I1972'],'C')
addclaim(33,908,2,[25],'274–279','Paraphrase: the examples should come from finite products or finite limits.','The inhabited-object theory with all functions is neither a finite-limit theory with pointwise limits nor an ordinary equational theory. The other displayed theories require their own presentations.','false for the blanket reading','The two maps1⇉2 in nonempty sets have empty equalizer, outside the category. Nonempty sets have no initial object, whereas an equational variety has the free algebra on the empty set. The source cannot use one algebraic characterization for all six quotients.','Gabriel–Ulmer finite-limit theory and Lawvere variety characterizations, as stated by Lack–Power and Adámek–Lawvere–Rosický, provide the exact closure tests.',['LackPower2009','AdamekLawvereRosicky2003'],'C',event='refuted, with refined statement RF-SA23-groupes-033')
addclaim(34,908,2,[24,25],'153–160;280–288','Paraphrase: B_G, an arrow, an endomorphism, a pointing and binary-operation theories.','The group presheaf topos B_G classifies G-torsors, while its objects are G-sets. An unconstrained endomorphism has N-actions as Set models, but its classifying topos is the presheaf topos on its finite-limit syntactic category, not automatically B_N. Each listed small operation signature has a small cartesian presentation.','examples and questions; no completed quotient classification','Flat functors from a one-object group are torsors; the regular action condition follows from filteredness of the category of elements. Arbitrary N-actions include the empty action, whereas a flat action has a nonempty category of elements. Thus the two classifier interpretations differ. Operations and equations generate the finite-limit syntactic category.','SGA4 IV1.2 supplies the flat-functor classification; finite-limit syntactic presentations are Gabriel–Ulmer theory. Classification of all subtopoi is not claimed to be completed by these examples.',['SGA4I1972','LackPower2009'],'A')
addclaim(35,909,2,[34,35],'546–736','Paraphrase: the Lawvere generator conditions and split forks should characterize monadicity.','For U:A→C with a left adjoint, Beck’s theorem requires conservativity and creation/preservation of the relevant U-split coequalizers. The source’s numbered blank cannot be omitted. A split fork with its displayed identities is an absolute coequalizer.','partial proof; an explicit numbered condition is left blank','For a split fork X1⇉X0→Y with section and contracting map, any equalizing map factors through the section formula and uniqueness follows from the split epi. Every functor preserves these identities. Comparison with the monad is an equivalence under Beck’s full conditions, not solely from the generator and adjunction.','Riehl Theorem5.5.1 pp204–205 and Proposition5.5.8 pp208–209 give exact criteria. Adámek–Lawvere–Rosický Lemma2.1 identifies the finitely presentable regular projectives needed by the Lawvere-generator discussion.',['RiehlContextOnline','AdamekLawvereRosicky2003'])
addclaim(36,909,2,[35],'739–776','Paraphrase: coalgebras for π=f*f_* form a topos, and the comparison is an equivalence under a conservative inverse image.','For a geometric morphism f:B→B′, π=f*f_* is a left-exact accessible comonad on B. Its coalgebras form a Grothendieck topos; if f* is conservative, B′≃Coalg(B,π). The source’s B rather than B′ has the wrong type.','proof sketched with a source/target error','Dual Beck applies because f* preserves equalizers. For an accessible left-exact comonad, coalgebras are accessible, have finite limits created in B and all colimits created in B; the topos exactness axioms and a small generator follow. In the G-set example f_*(Y)=Set(G,Y), counit evaluates at1, and comultiplication is induced by group multiplication.','Beck’s dual criterion follows from Riehl5.5.1; the accessible-category/sketch theorem supplies smallness. The topos part is also the left-exact comonad form of Giraud’s theorem, reconstructed with explicit accessibility rather than asserted for arbitrary large comonads.',['RiehlContextOnline','LackTendas2023','SGA4I1972'])
addclaim(37,909,2,[35],'778–808','Paraphrase: can a topos be embedded in one with sufficiently many points? The earlier fragment also mentions group cohomology.','Every Grothendieck topos Sh(C,J) is a geometric subtopos of PSh(C), whose evaluation points form a conservative set. The detached cohomology expression at540–544 does not determine a separate theorem.','open embedding question; detached formula underdetermined','The inclusion of sheaves has left-exact sheafification as left adjoint. Presheaf maps are isomorphisms iff all component maps are; each evaluation preserves colimits and finite limits and has a right adjoint. This is the requested inclusion direction, not a surjective cover from a point-rich topos.','SGA4 IV1.2 and its sheaf presentation settle the inclusion question. Searches of the adjacent monadic/group-action framework did not determine the degree or coefficient in the detached cohomology formula; it remains an identification flag.',['SGA4I1972'],'A')

addproblem(9,'Universal mixed envelope and the missing successor operation',[occurrence(1,list(range(4,11)),'5–263'),occurrence(1,[16],'436–461','wholly canceled repetition'),occurrence(2,list(range(27,33)),'294–535')],'Construct K, prove the filtered-colimit lemma and finish preexactness.','Bounded small sketches admit term constructions at a regular bound; the full mixed doctrine requires every successor obligation and compatibility condition.','C022–025 separate the regularity repair, the cylinder/fold construction and the conditional exact-cone lifting theorem. Lack–Tendas4.4/5.12 covers small sketches, not an unspecified large doctrine.',['LackTendas2023','Kelly1982'],worth='A formal reconstruction of the precise bounded doctrine is worthwhile; first make the unfinished preexactness definition explicit without treating it as manuscript text.')
addproblem(10,'Faithfulness tests as extension axioms',[occurrence(1,[11,12,13,14],'278–416')],'Can full faithfulness and faithfulness be encoded by essential-surjectivity conditions, compatibly with composition?','Yes by small glued cylinders and folds, with inherited marked cones and the stated compatibility.','C024 supplies the exact construction and proof; intersections and composition are direct consequences.',['Kelly1982','LackTendas2023'])
addproblem(11,'Is the displayed localization geometric?',[occurrence(2,[21,23],'43–77;127–148')],'The diagram asks loc? after a quotient of terms.','Ordinary quotient restriction is fully faithful; left exactness of its left adjoint requires additional hypotheses.','C028 gives the precise distinction and a finite-site counterexample to fullness of sheafification. SGA IV9 characterizes geometric localizations.',['SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(12,'Representably fully faithful maps of topoi',[occurrence(1,[19],'520–524')],'Does a fully faithful inclusion of represented theories come from a subtopos?','Yes when full faithfulness includes all 2-cells for every test topos.','SGA IV Proposition9.1.4 and Exercise9.1.6(b,c), original printed433–434, prove exactly C029.',['SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(13,'Exactness properties of model categories and a converse',[occurrence(2,[24],'161–174')],'Do finite limits in all model categories force a cartesian presheaf classifier?','Pointwise creation plus geometric-change compatibility is the relevant strong condition; bare existence is insufficiently specified here.','C027 distinguishes the proved forward result from the searched converse; Makkai–Pitts3.3/3.4 and Beke’s criteria are exact nearby results.',['MakkaiPitts1987','BekePresheafType','LackTendas2023'],worth='Worth a focused theorem-identification run using a fixed definition of the model-category limits and their underlying-object functors.')
addproblem(14,'Six elementary quotient theories and their lattice',[occurrence(2,[24,25],'179–264')],'Identify the displayed quotients, the subterminal classifier and complement relations.','The Sierpinski classifier is exact; six examples do not classify all quotients.','C030–031 gives the finite-cardinality counterexample to exhaustiveness and the open/closed distinction. The full lattice is larger than the drawn diagram.',['SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(15,'Constant and locally constant objects as theories',[occurrence(2,[25],'265–279')],'Can these classes be treated by the same algebraic/geometric scheme?','Not as full geometric subtheories with all ambient maps in arbitrary topoi.','C032 gives explicit Cantor-space filtered-colimit counterexamples; C033 separates nonempty objects from equational varieties.',['SGA4I1972','LackPower2009'])
addproblem(16,'Group, endomorphism and operation classifiers',[occurrence(2,[24,25],'153–160;280–288')],'What is represented by B_G and how do the listed operation theories fit?','B_G represents torsors; an endomorphism theory represents arbitrary N-actions and has its own cartesian classifier.','C034 checks the exact point/model distinction using SGA IV1.2. The page does not classify all quotient theories of these signatures.',['SGA4I1972','LackPower2009'])
addproblem(17,'Missing Beck condition and the comonadic topos',[occurrence(2,[34,35],'546–776')],'Finish the generator/monadicity conditions, the blank condition2 and the coalgebra comparison.','Use Beck’s split-coequalizer criterion and its dual, with accessibility for the coalgebra topos.','C035–036 gives the full hypotheses and corrects the B/B′ source. Riehl5.5.1/5.5.8 are exact opened references.',['RiehlContextOnline','LackTendas2023','SGA4I1972'])
addproblem(18,'A point-rich ambient topos and the detached cohomology formula',[occurrence(2,[35],'778–808'),occurrence(2,[34],'540–544','underdefined detached formula')],'Can a topos be geometrically included in a topos with enough points?','Yes via any small presheaf presentation; the cohomology degree/coefficient cannot be recovered locally.','C037 proves the inclusion direction. No stronger cover or identification of the detached formula is asserted.',['SGA4I1972'])

